WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.740
- Hi, I'm Kristine, co-owner
and self-taught baker

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.933
at Creme Caramel LA.

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.590
And today, I'm going to show you

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.070
how to take a traditional
Filipino dessert,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.910
and turn it to one of our
best sellers at our store.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.640
At Creme Caramel LA, we are
a Filipino dessert bakery.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.270
We make creme caramels,
bread puddings, custard pies,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.750
cheesecakes, quiches.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.850
But most of all, we make custard dreams.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.420
And today I'm going to
show you how to make

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.600
Ube upside-down pie.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.460
And it all happened
because of an accident.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.890
I don't know how many times
you've been in the kitchen where

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.750
realized, "oh no, I
forgot to set the timer!",

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.130
and basically turned a
whole day's work into crap.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.580
But I made a Graham cracker
crust, threw it on there,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.520
and it ended up being our
best seller at our store.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.760
Today, that's where we're going to make.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:50.860
Ube upside-down pie.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.450
So let's bring my
traditions to your table.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:55.650
Our first step is to make the custard.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.090
So we're going to get
started with two eggs,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.240
and we're going to separate
the yolks from the whites.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.040
After I crack them, I
like to use my hands.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.910
It does get a little slimy.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.570
Typically in leche flan,
it's purely egg yolks.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.010
But our version has egg whites

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.980
to make it a little silkier and creamier.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.760
You're probably wondering, "wait a minute.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.370
I've made this before and
it's not called leche flan!".

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.020
It's probably called creme caramel,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.060
which is the name of our company.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.170
That is also could be
called caramel pudding,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.220
or purin,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.130
or even creme Catalana.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.700
Those are all basically
the same custard base.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.390
So custard is basically
anything with eggs or milk.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.730
You can even make a savory
custard like a quiche.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.030
But our version is sweet, and
our version has thinned milk.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.890
So that's what makes it
implicitly leche flan,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.930
the Filipino version.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.043
Next, I'm going to add the single egg.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.150
You want to make sure
it's fully incorporated.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:47.270
Next we're going to add

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.000
six tablespoons of granulated sugar into

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.493
the egg mixture.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.870
And then now we're going
to whisk it all together.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.780
After our sugar and our
eggs are well-mixed,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.030
we're going to put in 18 ounces
of thinned evaporated milk.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.770
In the Philippines, fresh
milk isn't as available.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:07.860
It's a hot country.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.500
So using evaporated milk is much better,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:11.640
since it's shelf stable,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.177
and you don't have to
worry about spoiled milk.

00:02:13.177 --> 00:02:15.150
And next we're going to add
sweetened condensed milk,

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.770
and you're only using half a
cup of this condensed milk.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.660
And you want to make sure
you use a really good spatula

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.540
to kind of get all those little bits out.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.700
There's a word in Filipino
language called "sayang",

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.960
basically, it's like, "oh,
what a waste? What a pity."

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.120
And you don't want to waste it.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.840
If there's one thing
that we do at our store,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.920
we're really good about making sure

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.991
that we have every little bit used.

00:02:35.991 --> 00:02:36.824
Sayang.

00:02:36.824 --> 00:02:39.320
So we're done with the custard,
but what sets ours apart

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.800
is that we add ube to it.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.633
Woo!

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:43.810
If you don't, like, know what ube is,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:44.970
it is a party in your mouth.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.050
Ube is a purple yam from the Philippines,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.060
specifically indigenous
to the Philippines.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.150
The Ube flavor profile is
very similar to an almost

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.370
vanilla and coconut.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.150
We can debate ube all day.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.320
I mean, people say ube
is taro. No, it's not.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.730
Ube is a purple potato.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:02:59.563
No, it's not.

00:02:59.563 --> 00:03:01.410
Everyone thinks Ube tastes different.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.030
What do you guys think? Comment below.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.970
You can source this from your
local Filipino grocery stores.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.230
But if you don't have one near you,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.230
you can always go online
and purchase it there.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:13.000
So I'm going to add one
tablespoon of ube flavoring

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.170
into this beautiful custard.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.470
Look at that.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.260
Purple goodness, vibrant
royal purple color.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.280
We're not using powders or actual ube root

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.460
because it's going to ruin the texture.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:28.270
And we want to be able to stay
true to that texture of flan

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:31.490
as being creamy, and silky,
and really good mouthfeel.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.940
Now that we finished whisking
this beautiful purple custard,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.110
we're going to strain it.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.470
It's actually one of the biggest
complaints about custard.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.050
People don't like eggy custard,

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.570
or has like texture of eggs.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:42.450
As you can see here,

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.040
all these little eggy
bits are left behind,

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.100
and they're no longer going to interfere

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.687
with your beautiful silky custard.

00:03:48.687 --> 00:03:50.940
And we're going to put
the ube custard first.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.720
You know what, purple mess all over,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.113
just inside of the pie pan.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.440
We are going to scrape every little bit.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.273
You don't want sayang.

00:03:59.273 --> 00:04:00.106
Sayang.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.490
You want to use every little bit.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.550
And you're probably wondering

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.720
why our pie dish is in a roasting pan.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:06.830
And the reason why is

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.200
'cause we're going to
slow and low cook this.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.400
We're going to add a
water bath at the bottom.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.870
Basically, it's going to
make the custard cook evenly.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:17.120
We're going to fill up the hot water,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.810
halfway up the pan, as
best as you can estimate.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.850
You definitely don't
want it close to the lip

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.830
of the pie pan, 'cause it'll spill inside

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.200
when you're transporting
this into the oven.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.240
We already have a start with the hot water

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.840
going into the roasting pan.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.230
I'd like to add

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.930
a cover on top of the pan to create steam.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.250
So again, we want consistent custard.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.630
'Cause like a true leche flan,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.220
you want to make sure that
it's consistent throughout.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.430
And if this didn't have the foil on top,

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.850
it'll make a little crust,
and that's not very good

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.510
when it comes to the final product.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.750
We want to make a crust,
not this kind of crust.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.160
So I'm going to put this in the oven

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.680
for 45 minutes at 350 degrees.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.240
And then after 45 minutes,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.790
I'm going to remove the
foil and turn it around,

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.370
and bake it for another 10 to 15 minutes.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.750
And here is the little purple beauty.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.050
You just took this out of the oven.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.220
So how do you know that
it's completely set?

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.400
If you do a little jiggle,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.240
you'll see that it has a slight jiggle.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.570
So we're going to have a chill overnight

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.300
in the refrigerator, and
I'll see you the morning.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.740
Good morning.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.090
I am wearing the same thing.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:20.910
And that's okay. Please forgive it.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.330
We are ready for the next part,

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.400
which is our Ube upside-down
pie, Graham cracker crust.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.350
The reason why we call
it Ube upside-down pie

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.010
is because we have the crust on top.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.690
And the crust came about
because I made a mistake,

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:33.936
and I wanted to save it.

00:05:33.936 --> 00:05:35.780
The whole thing of sayang.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.700
So we're going to measure out

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.570
three and a half ounces
of Graham cracker crumbs.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.820
So now we're going to add brown sugar.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.310
The brown sugar provides a
little molasses-y type of

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:46.720
caramel-y sugar.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.270
Next I'm going to add some Greek yogurt,

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.630
whole milk yogurt.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.260
Again, you don't want to waste,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.180
so I'm scraping every little bit out.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.040
I grabbed some Graham cracker crumbs,

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.760
and I had Greek yogurt and brown sugar.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.380
So I just mix it all together.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.270
And I'm like, this tastes
fine. This tastes good.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:03.590
And it's sold out.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.150
It's sold out that following weekend.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.450
And I'm like, "oh my gosh,
we have a new product."

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.620
Don't be afraid of your mistakes.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.570
Your mistakes can always be
made into something else.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.960
To be honest, to make a
really consistent crumb,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:13.793
I like using my hands.

00:06:13.793 --> 00:06:15.950
So I'm going to get in there
and kind of crumble it.

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.260
It's like playing in sand,
except delicious, delicious sand.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:20.770
And if you crush it in your hands,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:22.310
it kind of has a little shape,

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.130
but then you can easily break it down.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:26.260
That's the right amount of moisture.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.250
So now we're going to add our crusty crust

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.240
onto the Ube custard.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.130
And then, push it all the way to the side,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.630
so you cover the crust, or
you can be a little delicate.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.220
I'm going to be a little
fancy into a little circle.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.850
After you smoothed out the crust,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.340
we're going to add some granulated sugar.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.500
Very light sprinkling, not
super heavy, just enough,

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.940
so that it lightly dusts
the top of the crust.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.420
After we sprinkled the sugar,

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.050
we're going to brulee
the surface of the crust.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.590
And not everyone has a torch at home,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:54.630
and that's completely fine.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.880
We're going to stick this into the broiler

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.100
for one to two minutes.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:58.963
To the boiler!

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:01.060
We are done.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.600
So we had this nice smoky finish on top.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.760
I'm going to add a little
purple sugar for a little jush.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:07.970
The reason why we put this on

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.090
is because we have other upside-down pies.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:11.810
So this is the indicator for us.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.640
That is our number one
dessert at our store,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:15.700
Ube upside-down pie.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.490
Purple yam custard, topped with
the bruleed Graham cracker,

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:19.900
Greek yogurt crust.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.430
I'm ready to sample this.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.650
I like to put it in a container

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.693
that you can see the purple goodness.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:28.540
I patting myself on the
back for making this,

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.200
like, "oh yeah, this is so delicious."

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.230
The Ube custard has that
vanilla-y slash coconut-y

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:35.680
earthy type of flavor,

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.170
married with the
creaminess of the custard.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:41.220
And then as a bonus,
you get a crumbly crust.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.410
Anyone can make this. You
can make this with your kids.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.690
You can make it with your friends.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:45.860
You get two things in one,

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.040
delicious food and Instagrammable pic.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.610
And that's how you turn a
traditional Filipino dish

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.240
into an Ube upside-down pie.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.540
We welcome you to come
by Creme Caramel LA.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.100
Try our Ube upside-down pie in person,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.080
paired with one of our ube horchatas.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.180
Thanks for joining me,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.593
as I bring my traditions to your table.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.353
- Oh yes!

